variants.reconstruction:
%n epiš | qerub | ... [... | damqat]
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “Pettinato translates the first word ‘si allontanò’ (i.e., @i{ibīš}), but his solution is rejected here on the grounds that this passage should be Enkidu’s explanation of Gilgameš’s first dream, and thus direct speech. In the light of @i{qerub} I assume @i{i-pí-iš} is the stative @i{epiš}; compare Enkidu’s chiastic warning, after the third dream, in OB Nippur obv. 2: @i{šunātum qitrubā aruḫ tāḫāzum}.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Nippur" 2

%n šuttaka | ihdu | īli[ṣ | libbašū-ma]
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 23
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 24

%n [p]ānāšu | itt[amrā]
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “i 3'–4'. These lines are a counterpart of OB Schøyen₂ 23–4: @i{damqat Gilgāmeš šuttašu iḫdu} / @i{īliṣ libbašūma pānūšu ittamrū}. Here @i{pa-na-šu}, normally a spelling of the accusative singular, is evidently nominative dual (@i{pānāšu}), analogous with @i{rēšān}, ‘head’.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 24

%n [i]ṣṣabtūni | illakūni
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “With @i{iṣṣabtūni illakūni} compare SB VI 168: @i{iṣṣabtūnimma illakūni} and, less nearly, SB III 19–20: @i{iṣṣabtūma} … @i{illakū}. This line would seem to cover the whole interval between the first dream episode and the second, omitting the elaborate details of march and preparation for sleep that occupy the late text (SB IV 34–47). Such brevity recalls the Old Babylonian tablet that covers this episode, from which the end of the line is accordingly restored (see OB Schøyen₂ 82).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 168

%n nubat[ta | iskipū | inīlū]
// L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 82
// cf. L I.4 SB "III" 85

%n šittu | rāhīt (|) mūši | iktaldaš[ššu | gilgāmeš]
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “Cf. OB Schøyen₂ 30: @i{šittum raḫiʾat nišī imqussu} and SB IV [15] and parallels: @i{šittum rēḫât nišī elīšu imqut}. At the end one might instead restore @i{šu-a-ti}, but there is a need for Gilgameš’s name somewhere in this passage.”
// L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 30
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 15

%n ina mišil (|) mušīti || iddīšu | šitta[šu]
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “The expression is curious; perhaps @i{id-dì-šu} is a corruption of @i{iddeki}, ‘he was roused (from sleep)’.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 2
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 31

%n [itbē-ma?] | šutta || izzaqqar | ana enkīdu
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 3
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 32

%n kī lā tadkânni | mīnâ[m | ērēku]
#note: See @bib{RN117@322}: “i 9' // 11'. Restored from SB IV [18] and parallels: @i{ibrī ul talsânni ammīni erēku}.”
// L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 4b
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 18
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 51
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 96
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 138
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 179

%n enkīdu | ana šuāti | izza[qqar]
#note: See @bib{RN117@323}: “Most read @i{ana ili šuʾāti} but Hecker’s position is more sensible: ‘Daß Enkidu zu einem @i{Gott} gesprochen hätte, wäre im Kontext zumindest problematisch’ (p. 669, fn. 20).”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 13a

%n [ul huršān] | mimma | nukkur
#note: See @bib{RN117@323}: “i 22'–3'. The phrase @i{mimma nukkur} occurs with words transposed in OB Harmal₁ 11 and OB Schøyen₂ 15–16.”
// L I.4 OB "Harmal₁" 11
// L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 15

%n [...] | alka | muʾʾi | puluhta[ka | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@323}: “The only point of contact between this line and the older text is the word @i{puluḫtu}: OB Schøyen₂ 20 has @i{ulawwa puluḫtašu birkīka}. What MB Boǧ₂ offers here is not obviously a corruption of that line and may report a different tradition. I follow von Soden’s parsing of @i{muḫḫi} (@bib{LS304@221}; @i{AHw} s.v. @i{maʾû}, now @i{muʾʾû} B in @i{CAD}); cf. Labat, ‘fustige la peur [que tu en as eue]’; Bottéro, ‘rejette tes craintes’; Tournay and Shaffer, ‘écarte la peur (que tu en as)’. Von Soden himself restored @i{pu-luḫ-ta-}[@i{šu}], ‘seine (des Berges?) Furchtbarkeit’ (cf. now OB Schøyen₂). @i{CAD} tries @i{muʾʾû} A: ‘praise(?) his fearfulness’ (M/2, p. 321, so also Hecker). Pettinato suggests @i{muḫḫi} for @i{ina muḫḫi}, ‘per ciò la paura’. More evidence is needed to secure a decipherment.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 20

%n ... | ina bīt (|) ilīšu | unassis | [qimmassu (...)]
#note: See @bib{RN117@323}: “Cf. SB VI 1–2: @i{imsi malêšu ubbib tillêšu} / @i{unassis qimmassu elu ṣērīšu}. The ‘temple of his god’, @i{ina} (or @i{ib-}) @i{bīt ilīšu}, is probably not to be taken seriously. It looks like a corruption under dictation (Hörfehler) of the words @i{ubbib tillêšu}, which, as the later text shows, belong to the previous line.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 2

%n ... | ittadi! | arš[ūtīšu | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@323}: “Comparison with SB VI 2–3 suggests that these three words are a corruption of @i{elu ṣērīšu} / @i{iddi} (@i{m})@i{aršūtīšu}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 3

%n lū <aššat>ka | anāku | lū m<ūtī> | <attā> | <šumma> (|) tahhazann[i | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “As it stands the extant text does not appear to have a counterpart in the later text. Thompson’s reading @i{ka-e-na ba-lum} made poor sense, even with the first word emended to @i{ka-ia-na} by von Soden (@bib{LS304@221}: ‘es sei(en) ständig’). I assume instead that what we have is corrupt: @i{lū} <@i{aššat}>@i{ka anāku lū m}<@i{utī atta šumma}> @i{taḫḫazanni}. Cf. Ištar’s proposal in SB VI 9: @i{atta lū mutīma anāku lū aššatka}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 9

%n ... | pappardilû? | ... [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “The first word is perhaps a simple error for @i{qí-iš-ša}, for the late text’s @i{qīšamma} (SB VI 8). Von Soden takes the stone name as @i{nirpappardillû} (@i{AHw}, p. 793) but the sign after @akk{NA₄} is hardly @akk{nir₂(|ZA.GIN₃|)} or any homophone of it.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 8

%n agalī! | rabûti | ušakl[al | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “vi 6'–10'. This is all very different from the later text (SB VI 9–21) but @i{agalī}(!) @i{rabûti} compares with @i{kudānī rabûti} (SB VI 12).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 12

%n išmē-ma | ištar || ša gilgāmeš | q[abâšu (...)]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “This line is the equivalent of SB VI 80: @i{Ištar annâ ina} [@i{šemêša}]. The entire reply of Gilgameš, which in the later text occupies 58 lines but would presumably have been considerably shorter at Boǧazköy, has been inadvertently omitted, perhaps by skipping from one ruling to the next.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 80

%n šēṭat | sikkatu | ekketa | [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “vi 12'–14'. If @i{šēṭat} takes the place of the late text’s @i{uggugat} (SB VI 81) and @i{ēli ana šamê} the place of @i{ana šamāmī} [@i{īli}] (end of the same line), the diseases and the pot must represent a detail which did not survive the transmission. The diseases also appear together in an OB incantation (Böhl, @i{Leiden Coll.} II, p. 3, 13: @akk{ek-[ke]-tam si₂-ik-ka-tam}, as read by @i{CAD} S, p. 251) but it is difficult to see them as appropriate in the present context. Perhaps one should consider the extra words a corrupt tradition (@i{šiggata} < *@i{šēgugat}, ‘furious’, @i{ek-ke-ta} < @i{eqqet}, ‘tongue-tied’?). On the other hand, given the presence of @i{pursītu} in the next line, perhaps @i{ši-ig-ga-ta} represents @i{šikkatu}, ‘flask’. In l. 13' the traces do not support Tournay and Shaffer’s @i{sa-ḫi-i}[@i{r-ti} (@bib{RN580@158, fn. 68}).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 81

%n gilgāmeš | [lunār | ina šubtīšu?]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “The end of this line is restored from SB VI 95: @i{Gilgāmeš lunerru ina šubtīšu}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 95

%n šumma | <alâ | lā tanaddin>am | ... [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@324}: “@i{šum-ma-am} is perhaps corrupt for @i{šumma} <@i{alâ lā tanaddin}>@i{am} (cf. SB VI 96).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 96
